2 CONT
2 CONT Inventory of Dame Louis Juchereau de St. Denis:  Taken from "My Historic Natchitoches, Louisana and Its Environment" by Louis Raphael Nardini, Sr. pg 77
2 CONT
2 CONT 		April 24, 1758 - Natchicotches Courthouse Records.
2 CONT
2 CONT 		Inventory of Dame Louis Juchereau de St. Denis' possessions
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Today, I, Francois Langlois, Corporal of the militia and assigned to Post St. Jean Baptiste de Natchitoches as clerk of this post, acting on the orders of Sieur Charles Louis de Blanc, Commandant of this post and executor of affa
2 CONC irs both civil and military, obtained the seals from the hands of Louis La Malathie called "Joubert", merchant of this post, accompanied by Sieur Danial du Pain notary and sub-delegate, in charge of inventories of this post, Sieur
2 CONC  Remis (Remi) Poissot, sergant of the militia of this post, and Guilleume Lestage, corporal of the militia and assistant interpreter of Indian language of this post.  The said four, La Malathie, du Pane, Poissot and Lestage, havin
2 CONC g affixed the said seals at the house of Dame St. Denis the Sunday morning previous to the date April 17, 1758.  With the seals in hand and in the presence of the above witnesses and before the following witnesses:  Sieur Louis Ch
2 CONC arles de Blanc Witness for his heirs grandchildren of Dame St. Denis; Sieur Athanase de Mezieres, witness for his heir, granddaughter of Dame St. Denis; Louis Charles Juchereau de St. Denis, son of Dame St. Denis; Marie des Neigas
2 CONC , wife of Mannel De Soto and daughter of Dame de St. Denis; Marie de Blanc, wife of the above post commandant and daughter of Dame de St. Denis, the inventory was made.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	We began by opening a walnut armor, with lock and key and found the following:  1 blue damask cloth shirt, lined, 1 blue shirt of cotton cloth, another blue shirt of cotton cloth, another blue shirt of the same material, 1 black 
2 CONC cape with white stripes and embroidered gold flowers, and 1 white linen apron.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	In a cypress chest we found:  6 embroidered table cloths, 4 napkins of the same material and design, 7 silver spoons, 7 silver forks, 1 small spoon.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	There was another small cypress chest with lock and key with nothing in it.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	In a small safe with lock and key was found an extract of records of the secession of the late de St. Denis of the Supreme Councile of Louisiana Dated August 9, 1756 concerning the heirs of St. Denis and the receipts made after t
2 CONC he sharing September 20, 1756 in accordance with the decree of the Council.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	In another cypress chest nothing was found.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	In a pantry with lock and key was found:  3 silver spoons and 3 silver forks, 1 large silver ladel, 1 china cup, 16 china saucers, 1 china sugar bowl, 1 china soap dish with cover, 1 china gravy ladle, 9 large china dishes, 17 ch
2 CONC ina plates, 2 china salad dishes, 2 china feeding bottles, 2 china water jugs, small 2 silver candle sticks, 2 china bottles, one for oil and one for vinegar, 1 small chest in which was a small Bible, written in Spanish, and a tin
2 CONC y gold cross with a Christs.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Found in the office:  1 empty chest of oak and lock and key, 5 chairs-3 in good conditions, 2 arm chairs, 1 wooden arm chair, 1 wooden bed with feather mattress.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	The house is 22 feet long-19 feet wide with its galleries surrounded with cedar columns 12 feet high on cypress legs, same height as those of the gallery.  Behind the house is a shed 52 feet long and 18 feet wide, closed at each 
2 CONC end by rooms 12 x 18 feet.  Inside the shed we found a carriage with a set of double harnesses, 1 burrow.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	There was a baking house and kitchen with a large kitchen range and two demijohns with water, two tables, 2 chairs, 4 iron posts - 2 small and 2 large - and 1 pantry with double doors with iron work and latch.  The baking house a
2 CONC nd kitchen is 14 x 14 feet with front and back galleries in poor condition.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	There was a chicken house 18x10 feet on cypress logs, covered with bark.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Also there was a Negro house 14x14 feet with front gallery and covered with bark so that it would not leak when it rained.  The heirs declaire that all within the house belongs to the Negress, who is free and called "Octavine."
2 CONT
2 CONT 	On this same premisses we found a smithy's home 20x18 feet surrounded by cypress logs covered with bark.  At the side of the house was a shed closed on three sides which measured 20 feet in each direction.  Inside this shed was f
2 CONC ound: a smith's anvil, a beaked anvil, an old vise which is broken, a forge and bellows, and four smith's hammers of different sizes.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	All of the above, by their signatures, declare this inventory to be just and final with nothing being omitted or overlooked.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Done this day and year in Natchitoches, April 24, 1758.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Signed:  Louis de Blanc; Francois Langlois; Louis LaMalthie; Remi Poissot; Guilleume Lestage; L.C. J. de St. Denis; Athanase Demeziere; Marie de Blanc; Marie Neigas de Soto; Danial du Pain as witness.  Danial du Pain, notary.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	On the same date as in the previous document the following decision was made and agreed upon in front of themselves and the witnesses undersigned.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	It is agreed then, by the undesigning heirs of the late Dame L.J. de St. Denis, that I, Louis Caesar de Blanc, Knight of the Royal and Military Order of St. Louis, Commandant of the post des Natchitoches in the Province of Louisi
2 CONC ana, proceed in compliance with: Manuel DeSoto on behalf of his wife, Marie des Neigas Juachereau de St. Denis; Athanase de Meziere on behalf of his daughter, granddaughter of the deceased Dame St. Denis; Louis Charles de St. Deni
2 CONC s, son of the deceased; I, Louis Ceasar de Blanc on behalf of my wife, Marie de Blanc, daughter of the deceased; all who are heirs of the late lady, Marie Manuello Sanchez Navarro Y Romone de St. Denis, ask that all the inventory,
2 CONC  possessions of the deceased be auctioned off and that the amount of the proceeds of the sale be shared by all concerned.  They ask that witnesses and leaders of this place be chosen to represent de La Chaise's son, Antoine St. De
2 CONC nis de la Chaise, heir and grandson of the deceased Dame L. J. de St. Denis, and that justice be made at Natchitoches this date April 24, 1758.
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed:  de Blanc, Commandant:
2 CONT
2 CONT August 1, 1758.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	We the undersigned, heirs of the secession of the late Dame St. Denis as written in the previous document, accept the condition of the extract of April 24, 1758, concerning the terms of the allotment and sale of the property of d
2 CONC eceased Madame L.J. de St. Denis.  Signed at Natchitoches this date August 1, 1758.
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed:  With permission granted, de Blanc signs for the son of de la Chaise; Louis de St. Denis, son; Marie Rose de Blanc, daughter; Marie de St. Denis De Soto, daughter; LeCheve. Jean Louis Caesar de Blanc, Commandant; Daniel du
2 CONC  Pain, notary.
2 CONT
2 CONT From The Handbook of Texas Online:
2 CONT
2 CONT ST. DENIS, MANUELA SÁNCHEZ NAVARRO DE (1697-1758). Manuela Sánchez Navarro y Gomes Mascorro, the daughter of Diego Sánchez Navarro y Camacho and Mariana Gomes Mascorro y Garza, was baptized in Monclova, Coahuila, Mexico, in 1697. 
2 CONC Despite never having lived in Texas, she figures as prominently as any other woman in the early history of the region. Her paternal grandmother, Feliciana Camacho y Botello, was married first to Diego Sánchez and afterward to Comm
2 CONC andant Diego Ramón.qv When Louis Juchereau de St. Denisqv arrived on July 19, 1714, at San Juan Bautistaqv Presidio (at the site of present-day Guerrero, Coahuila), Ramón placed him under house arrest in compliance with a 1713 vic
2 CONC eregal order prohibiting entry of foreign traders or their merchandise into Spanish territory. Word of the Frenchman's presence was forwarded to the viceroy, who did not respond until the following March. During the intervening mo
2 CONC nths of St. Denis's detention he became engaged to Manuela. News of the impending union reached the viceroy in Mexico City before St. Denis arrived there to face charges and defend himself. He no doubt knew his engagement to Manue
2 CONC la would enhance the chances for his successful outcome in Mexico, since the viceroy knew that Manuela was directly descended from several of the military, political, and financial elite of New Spain. After St. Denis's exoneration
2 CONC , on August 22, 1715, the general junta appointed him conductor of supplies for the planned Ramón expeditionqv to Texas. Under the command of Domingo Ramón,qv the commandant's son, the 1716 expedition was instructed to reestablish
2 CONC  Spanish missionsqv in East Texas. This expedition was the first step in permanent Spanish occupation of lands northeast of the Rio Grande. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The year of Manuela's departure from New Spain to join her husband is uncertain. Whether she joined him first in Mobile, Alabama, or Natchitoches, Louisiana, also is a unknown. Earliest census records for Poste St. Jean Baptiste d
2 CONC es Natchitoches, where St. Denis assumed command in 1721, indicate that the first two of the couple's children were born in New Spain. Manuela could have joined St. Denis in the French colony as early as 1719 but no later than 172
2 CONC 1. They eventually had two sons and five daughters, including Marie Petronille Feliciane, who married Athanase de Mézièresqv on April 18, 1746. Whatever problems existed to keep St. Denis out of New Spain, they were resolved by 17
2 CONC 43. In January of that year he wrote the French crown requesting permission to resign his command at Natchitoches, retire from the crown's service and, with his wife and minor children, move to New Spain. Months later his request 
2 CONC to retire was granted, but his request to move to New Spain was denied. To compensate him for the rebuff, the French crown assured St. Denis that his children would be placed as advantageously as possible in the royal service. St.
2 CONC  Denis died in Natchitoches on June 11, 1744. Manuela, a widow at age forty-seven with three or four minor children, remained in Natchitoches. She continued to live in the house St. Denis built for her on property now occupied by 
2 CONC the campus of Northwestern State University of Louisiana. She died in Natchitoches on April 16, 1758. The St. Denis children and their families played significant roles in the continuing development of Texas, Louisiana, and New Sp
2 CONC ain. 
2 CONT
2 CONT BIBLIOGRAPHY: Eugene P. Watson Library Archives, Northwestern State University, Natchitoches, Louisiana. Eugenio del Hoyo, Historia del Nuevo Reino de León, 1557-1723 (Monterrey, 1972). Immaculate Conception Church Archives, Natch
2 CONC itoches, Louisiana. Monclova Baptismal Records, Latter-day Saints Microfilm Library. Max L. Moorhead, The Presidio: Bastion of the Spanish Borderlands (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1975). Robert S. Weddle, San Juan Bautis
2 CONC ta: Gateway to Spanish Texas (Austin: University of Texas Press, 1968). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Patricia R. Lemée 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT On July 19, 1714, St. Denis strode into the Presidio San Juan Bautista Del Rio Grande de Norte, located in the state of Coahulia, Mexico. The Commander of the presidio, Major Diego Ramón, placed St. Denis under a pleasant house ar
2 CONC rest. The Spanish Crown had enacted an order prohibiting the entry of foreign traders or their merchandise into Spanish territory.  St. Denis violated the prohibition by bearing goods banned by Spanish mercantile restrictions.  Ma
2 CONC jor Ramón awaited instructions from Mexico City on what to do with this foreigner bearing such goods. In the interim, St. Denis, a Frenchman and a cavalier, and wasting no time, used the occasion to court, and win a promise of mar
2 CONC riage from Ramón's beautiful step-granddaughter, Dona Maria Manuela Sánchez Navarro y Gomez Mascorro.  Manuela was the daughter of Don Diego Sanchez Navarro y Camacho and Dona Mariana Gomez Mascorro de la Garza.  She was the grand
2 CONC daughter of Don Diego Sanchez Navarro and Dona Feliciana Camacho y Botello.  When widowed, Dona Feliciana married a second time to the Major Diego Ramon, the former Governor of Coahuila, and now the Commander of the Presidio.  St.
2 CONC  Denis, ordered to Mexico City, defended himself properly and competently.   Soon after, on July 17, 1716, he became a member of the Ramón Expedition. Appointed as commissary officer, his duties included the founding of Spanish mi
2 CONC ssions in East Texas.  This entrada  or expedition would represent Spain’s commitment to a permanent occupation of the province of Texas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This same year and on February 17, 1716, St. Denis had married Manuela in the local Chapel of the Presidio San Juan Bautista. During 1716 and 1717, he participated in the founding of six missions and a presidio in East Texas.  On 
2 CONC April 1717, he returned to San Juan Bautista with a sizable amount of merchandise. Although well received on his first visit, the era of Franco-Spanish cooperation ended with the death of King Louis XIV and thereby concluded the t
2 CONC hirteen years’ War of the Spanish Succession  (1701-1714).  St. Denis, again found himself viewed a foreigner under suspicion.  However, this time he underwent more severe repercussions. To avoid a transfer to Spain as a prisoner 
2 CONC when sent to Mexico City a second time, St. Denis fled the capital. By February 1719, he made his way back to Natchitoches. In 1721, Spanish officials permitted his wife, Manuela, to join him, and the couple spent their remaining 
2 CONC years at the French outpost on the Red River. The 1722 census for Natchitoches lists the St. Denis’ and two children. The 1726 census of Natchitoches, LA.  indexes St. Denis, his wife and three children. 
2 CONT
