2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Juan Bautista Chapa was born in Arbisola (sic: either Albisola or Albissola), a small village outside Genoa (about 35 kilometers west), in the Province of Tuscany (sic: Liguria) in Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT His parents were Bartolome Schiapapria and Batestina Badi. His parents held middle class standing and owned a vineyard. Bartolome’s two sons did their duty in the Italian wars, furnishing their own arms. Both young men came from a
2 CONC  very literate family and entertained being priests.
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan was ordained a priest in Spain. Juan Bautista vacillates. Juan Bautista studied for two years in Spain to the priesthood. At eighteen, he has a change of heart and decides to emigrate to New Spain. He brings in his possession
2 CONC  a bronze headpiece, a sword, a bronze pistol, a coat of mail. How he paid for his studies in Spain and passage is unknown. His parents probably contributed to the Church for his education and travel.
2 CONT
2 CONT De Leon who chronicled the events of Nuevo Leon from 1577 through 1635 did so by recording the oral testimony of witnesses that had been participants to many of the events that occurred before his arrival in New Spain. He describe
2 CONC d the history of Nuevo Leon, including the main players such as Diego de Montemayor, the founder of Monterrey.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Juan Bautista arrived in New Spain, he through happenstance met De Leon now an older man. De Leon had attempted to use his influence to get high officials in the Church to publish his writings, but to no avail. Chapa became f
2 CONC ascinated with De Leon and his work. Here De Leon met a man who really appreciated his literary efforts. They formed a lasting friendship and the results of that lasting friendship brought a record of the history of Nuevo Leon. Th
2 CONC e first fifty years written by the elder De Leon and complemented with the written history by Juan Bautista Chapa.
2 CONT
2 CONT In their early years, De Leon offered Chapa a clerical position which he readily accepted. Chapa rode out on all expeditions. He also performed ranch work and directed the work of others. He became familiar with Indians under his 
2 CONC supervision and participated in the expeditions to castigate them when the government felt it necessary to conduct acts against them for committing violent acts against Spanish settlers.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Governor Zavala met Chapa, he was much impressed by the young Italian and talked De Leon into letting Chapa become his Secretary. Even though the Secretary was a position appointed by the Crown, through diplomacy Zavala appoi
2 CONC nted the man of his choice, Chapa. From his initial appointment as Secretary to the Governor, Chapa served an uninterrupted forty years plus as the recorder of the time, kept contact with the outside world and held the office of s
2 CONC econd-in-command in governmental and war matters.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of Juan Bautista Chapa’s recorded events tells the story of the expedition formed to punish a band of Indians who had committed depredations against the Spaniards. Led by an Indian guide, after many hours of travel through den
2 CONC se brush the small force arrived at the Indian encampment at day break and immediately attacked, practicing a time honored Spanish custom of attack. While on this expedition, Chapa discovered later he had dropped his prized bronze
2 CONC  pistol.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his tenure as Secretary, Chapa served nine governors, taking leaves of absence to participate on major expeditions against the Indians and into Tejas to hunt the French. He made detailed maps and pinpointed major land featu
2 CONC res with observed longitude and latitude. Chapa is credited with the founding of the Village of Monclova.
2 CONT
2 CONT As an author he wrote the History of Nuevo Leon from 1650 to 1690. He did not enter his name in any way. Some 219 years, his manuscript was published in 1909, by another who gave credit to an "Unknown Author." Chapa gave good reas
2 CONC on in his manuscript for omitting his name, stating "because of the incredulity of some and the censure of others. I will not have to listen to the plaudits of friends or the harping of enemies, and if I remain unknown, they will 
2 CONC not have a target to shoot at." He wrote forty-five chapters over a forty plus time span, with a feather pen, bad ink, and almost by candlelight. And yet, in his last will and testament, you still can sense his need to have accomp
2 CONC lished more.
2 CONT
2 CONT A perusal of his last will and testament will give you a greater appreciation of the man and those things he considered important.
2 CONT
2 CONT LAST WILL AND TESTAMENT
2 CONT
2 CONT Testament of Juan Bautista Chapa, citizen of this Realm, legitimate son of Bartolome Schiapapria and Batestina Badi, natives of the Town of Arbisola in the rivera of Genoa. I instruct that I be buried as decided by my executors. I
2 CONC  do not have the wealth to have a mass Sung over my body. I hereby declare that I was married to Beatriz de Treviño, now dead. Sons: Nicholas, Gaspar, Jose Maria and Maria. My wife received as dowry. 500 goats and four caballerias
2 CONC  of land, and a law suit with Diego de Treviño, and in possession of Margarita Rodriguez, woman of Juan Conde. I declare that administrator of Juan de Olivares and of Juana de Treviño, my parents in law; that the latter died in Ju
2 CONC ly of 1693, and that I have not completed the trusteeship as the distribution, or partition is pending on the Estancia de San Antonio. I declare that I was administrator of the merchandise of the General Don Leon de Alga, of more 
2 CONC than 40,OOO pesos; that if anything is owed me I forgive it. That I also administered the hacienda of Don Nicholas de Azcarrage, valued at 48,OOO pesos. That he gave me a shirt all the years "for the assistance and diligence that 
2 CONC l offered his government". That if there is anything I owe him that he pardon me, as I have noted my poverty. Possessions: My house of four rooms, left to me by Olivares; a lot given me in appreciation by the Governor General Alon
2 CONC zo de Leon, before Pedro de Almandos, who gave twenty varas to Maria de Chapa, his granddaughter. Of the lots in the north, I gave one to Juan Garcia, one to Juan de Leon, and another to the daughter of Francisco, slave of the ben
2 CONC eficiary priest. Executors Gaspar and Jose de Chapa. I ask that they guard Juana and attempt to help her marry. That Nicolas has nine or ten horses in Cerralvo that I leave to him to work for the good of his soul and that of his w
2 CONC ife. Before the General Don Antonio Fernandez Vallejo, Lieutenant to the Governor. Witnesses: The Lieutenant Pedro de Almandos, Lieutenant Jose de Treviño, Lazaro de Avila, Jose Saenz and Tomas de Treviño Monterrey, 8th of Februar
2 CONC y 1694.
2 CONT
2 CONT CODICIL OF JUAN BAUTISTA CHAPA:
2 CONT
2 CONT Possessions - The Vineyard of my father, Bartolome Schiapapria, in Arbisola that I inherited together with my brothers, Nicholas and Francisco. It is said that Nicholas went to Cadiz in search of Juan Bautista his uncle, "and ther
2 CONC e took the habit" and that Francisco left. I have written my uncle, Juan, the youngest of the family, and have given him authority to use and benefit by it. This letter was written when I left for New Spain, which was in the year 
2 CONC 1647, and I have not heard from. The vineyard was worth 4OO esquedos.
2 CONT
2 CONT I also mark as possessions, one jinete saddle and rigging, some spurs, a bridle. Items: a well used curry comb, an old dagger, a bed of boards and sides and an old mattress, a blanket half -----, a bed sheet, and two pillows. One 
2 CONC table, a bench and three old stools. One writing desk, much misused, and a small trunk in which I keep my papers, a large chest and its key, and a chair with a back piece and a picture of St. Jerome. Also, Thirty head of cattle an
2 CONC d eight caballerias of land in the jurisdiction of Cerralvo (La Piedra Parada), two lots in the same town, two metates, a case of ten books, another small house, a headpiece of copper [helmet] which is to be given to Juana, two si
2 CONC lver spoons with my name and that of my Wife, one on each, which is to be given to Gaspar.
2 CONT
2 CONT I declare that Maria married Francisco de Treviño, and I gave her a dowry and that they give her the St. Francis and the virgin de Guadalupe.
2 CONT
2 CONT My clothes and forty books I leave to Gaspar. The Italian books to Francisco Capurro Risso, of Mexico, nephew of Juan de Vargas, surgeon; and the books in Latin, "some of which are very valuable," are to be sold and masses said fo
2 CONC r my soul.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of the caballerias in San Antonio of the Pesqueria Chica, that belonged to Juana de Olivares, one is to be given to Maria and the other to Juana, her daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before the Lieutenant of the Governor, the General Antonio Fernandez Vallejo. Witnesses: Pedro de Almandos, Lieutenant, the Lieutenant Jose Saenz and Lazaro de Avila. Monterrey, 18th of February, 1694
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan Bautista Schiapapria1 Chapa was born Abt 1624 in Albísola, Italy, and died Abt 1694 in Monterrey. He married Beatris Treviño Abt 1652 in Monterrey, daughter of Juan de Olivares and Juana de Treviño.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Juan Chapa and Beatris Treviño are:
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 i. Gaspar Treviño2 Chapa, born Abt 1654 in Monterrey. 3 ii. José Maria Treviño Chapa, born Abt 1655 in Monterrey. + 4 iii. Nicolas Chapa Treviño, born Abt 1655 in Monterrey. 5 iv. Maria Treviño Chapa, born Abt 1656 in Monterrey.
2 CONC  http://longoriaf.tripod.com/Chapa.htm#Generation No. 4
2 CONT
