1 OCCU Diego was named as an accomplice by Nicolás Ortiz, who stabbed Rosas to death on Jan 25, 1642. Ortiz claimed Diego encouraged him to kill Rosas. Governor Alonso de Pacheco y Heredia investigated the murder and sentenced those exec
2 CONC uted. See the article on
1 TITL Capitán
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Diego de Marquez'''
2 CONT
2 CONT   General Notes: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins of NM Families pages 107-108 (see sources)
2 CONT
2 CONT At this time New Mexico was divided into the Rio Ariba and the Rio Abajo. The Rio Ariba is the area north the lava cliffs and steep incline called La Bajada just south of Santa Fé. The Rio Abajo is the area south that included Alb
2 CONC urqueque. The Governor was in charge of the Rio Ariba and the Lieutenant Governor (Lieutenant General) was in charge of the Rio Abajo.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Noted events in his life and other information:
2 CONT
2 CONT • Dates & Events: 252  Diego de Trujillo first appears in New México as an Alférez and famer, nineteen or twenty years old, in 1632. He was a soldier-escort in 1641. In 1662, he was fifty, a Sargento Mayor, living in the jurisdict
2 CONC ion of Sandia as Lieutenant General for the Rio Abajo area, as well as Alcalde Mayor of Zuni. He then declared that he was born in México City. His wife was Catalina Vásquez.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1661, Governor Mendizábal confiscated his Zuni alcaldía. Many records can be found that tell of his troubles with this governor. From then on we learn that his home was four leagues from Sandia Pueblo, and that his two sons-in-
2 CONC laws were Andrés Hurtado and Cristóbal Baca. The name of his estancia was "Paraje de las Huertas." Diego gave his age as forty-eight in 1661. By 1669, he was Maese de Campo and also Syndic of the Franciscans at Sandia. His wife ag
2 CONC e her age as forty-eight at this time, and stated that she had been born in Santa Fé. Diego also served a short term as second Alcalde Mayor of Guadalupe del Paso.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1680, he gave his opinions about the cause and problems of the Indian Rebellion, but is not mention in the following year. He died at Casas Frande in 1682. He had one son, Francisco, who married to a daughter of María de Vera. 
2 CONC ONMF, pg. 108
2 CONT
2 CONT • Name: Diego is considered the Pregenitor of the Trujillo family of New Mexico. 250 
2 CONT
2 CONT • Web Reference: On the Beyond Origins site, some possible ancestory for Diego is addressed. 250 
2 CONT
2 CONT • Dates & Events: 250  In 1662, Captain Diego de Trujillo was identified as an encomendero in a suit that he brought against Governor don Berbardo López de Mendizábal, Trujillo, demanding to be paid 868 pesos by the governor for l
2 CONC ivestock and Apache servants. In his claim there was mention of his son, Captain Francisco de Trujillo, and two son-in-laws: Captain Cristóbal Baca (husband of Ana Moreno de Lara, aka Moreno de Trujillo) and Antonio de Carbajal. T
2 CONC his information indicates that Truijillo had a second daughter whose name is not known at this time, but who was the wife of Antonio de Carbajal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Diego married Catalina Marquez Vásquez, daughter of Diego Márquez and Bernardina Vásquez, about 1633 in Sandia, Bernalillo, Nuevo Méjico, Nueva España. (Catalina Marquez Vásquez was born in 1621 in Santa Fé, Santa Fé, Nuevo Méjico
2 CONC , Nueva España 252 and was christened in 1621 in Santa Fé, Military Chapel of Our Lady of Light (La Castrense), Santa Fé, Santa Fé, Nuevo Méjico, Nueva España.)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://cybergata.com/roots/5360.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT The major accomplice in the death of Governor Rosas. He was beheaded in 1643. His half-breed illegitimate son, Juan Márquez, 36 years old in 1639-40, an alferez and treasurer of the Holy Crusade was said to have been murdered by o
2 CONC rders of Governor Rosas, which accounts for Diego’s part in the Rosas Murder. He apparently was related to co-conspirators Cristóbal Enriques, who was also executed, and Agustín Carvajal. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  * BIRTH: ABT 1602, Nueva España [103196]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * DEATH: 21 JUL 1643, Santa Fe, New Mexico [103197] [103198] [103199]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * BURIAL: Santa Fe, La Castrense (military chapel), New Mexico
2 CONT
2 CONT     * EVENT: a woman from Santo Domingo by the name of Catiti.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Had child with: ABT 1630 [103200]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * EVENT: executed: 21 JUL 1643, Santa Fe, on the plaza, New Mexico [103201] [103202] [103203] 
2 CONT
2 CONT [103196] If Diego's mother was an unknown woman in New Mexico, as some have assumed, it is likely that he was born in New Mexico. If however Ana de Mendoza was his mother, which is more probable, he was born in Nueva España..
2 CONT
2 CONT [103197]Diego was named as an accomplice by Nicolás Ortiz, who stabbed Rosas to death on Jan 25, 1642. Ortiz claimed Diego encouraged him to kill Rosas. Governor Alonso de Pacheco y Heredia investigated the murder and sentenced th
2 CONC ose executed by beheading on the plaza. See Juan de Arechuleta for more details.
2 CONT
2 CONT [103201]Diego was named as an accomplice by Nicolás Ortiz, who stabbed Rosas to death on Jan 25, 1642. Ortiz claimed Diego encouraged him to kill Rosas. Governor Alonso de Pacheco y Heredia investigated the murder and sentenced th
2 CONC ose executed. See the article on Juan de Arechuleta for more details.
2 CONT
