1 NOTE
2 CONT Next, probably was your ancestor, MELCHORA, born c. 1658 and married c. 1676 to Joseph de Ornelas y Mendoza.
2 CONT 	A son was Hernando Pérez de Frías (named after Hernando de Frías), whose spouse was Maria de Carbajal y Yáñez.  Hernando was gone prior to May 1718, while his widow lived in Oct 1728.  Two sons married in San Felipe Cuquío,
2 CONC  Jalisco and settled there.
2 CONT 	The fourth and last identified offspring was Catalina Cortés, married in Nochistlán 11 Nov 1691 to "Lázaro Sánchez, español vecino de Jalostotitlán."  He was a son of Lázaro Sánchez and Jerónima Rodríguez de Chavarría.
2 CONC   The "padrinos" of the ceremony were "Pedro Ramírez y María Sánchez."  They were from Jalos; she was the groom's sister and Pedro was her husband.
2 CONT 	Have a marriage "dispensa" dated 7 May 1718 (6119, AGN) in which the family tree says Diego Pérez de Frías/Pérez Delgadillo had a sister, María Delgadillo.  She gave birth to Melchora de los Reyes, who married Nicolás Vargas
2 CONC .  Their marriage was in Nochistlán, 16 Apr 1654: "Nicolás Vargas con Melchora de los Reyes, hija legítima de María Delgadillo.  Fueron los padrinos Domingo de Lomelín y Juana de Mendoza."  As I've seen was the habit at that 
2 CONC early period, the parents of the betrothed who were deceased were not mentioned.  Found data on this couple's children living in San Felipe de Cuquío.
2 CONT 	In Nochistlán, 28 Apr 1631 "casé y velé a Bernardino Gonzalez hijo de Francisco González y Magdalena de Labesares su mujer, con Mariana de Sandoval hija de Juan de Sandoval y Catalina Cortés su mujer.  Fueron los padrinos Ge
2 CONC rónimo Gonzalez Aramburu y Elvira de Carvajal y Ulloa su mujer."  These were the parents of Catalina Cortés de Isla, who wed Diego Pérez de Frías/Pérez Delgadillo.
2 CONT 	Bernardino "and company" manufactured a lot of children, and most of them married.  His death, in ibid., 5 Jan 1681: "Murió de tabardillo en la Estancia de Nangue, Bernardino de Ysla, viudo de Mariana de Sandoval, españoles, a 
2 CONC la edad de 73 años.  (His birth: Jan 1681-73 1607) Testó el 23 de octubre de 1671 ante Melchor de Sandoval, siendo albacea su esposa, difunta y herederos sus hijos e hijas."
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Following up on Rodrigo de Carbajal and his spouse, MELCHORA DE LOS REYES, residents of Ahuacatlán, Nayarit.  Putting together my notes on the Carbajales, from various sources, has borne fruit.
2 CONT 	According to Fray Antonio Tello in his history of Nueva Galicia, one of the founders of Santiago de Compostela was Rodrigo de Carbajal y Ulloa.  Amaya Topete wrote that he was a miner and land owner in Tenango and the Autlán val
2 CONC ley, with residence in Ahuacatlán, where he took part in an auction in 1579.  In 1557, he was the "Alcalde Mayor" of the Provincia de Avalos.  He still lived on 5 Apr 1606, when he was the Godfather at a christening in Guadalajar
2 CONC a.
2 CONT 	His first wife and relative, born in Mexico City, was Bernardina de Tapia, the daughter of Antonio de Carbajal and Catalina de Tapia.  Their two sons, Agustín and Diego de Carbajal y Tapia became friars in the Order of San Agust
2 CONC ín.  I'll cite their records as Aspirantes a Cargos del Santo Oficio later on.
2 CONT 	Rodrigo later married Francisca de Figueroa y Temiño de Bañuelos, a daughter of Francisco de Figueroa y Carbajal and Mariana Temiño de Bañuelos y Alcocer.  Their offspring were:
2 CONT 1.	Rodrigo de Carbajal y Figueroa, married to Melchora de los Reyes.
2 CONT 2.	Francisca de Carbajal y Figueroa, married to Juan de Ocampo (residents of Colima).
2 CONT 3.	Ana de Carbajal y Figueroa married Francisco Tello de Orozco.  Died 12 May 1620 in Guadalajara.
2 CONT 4.	Isabel de Carbajal y Figueroa wed Gabriel del Aguila.
2 CONT 5.	Catalina de Carbajal y Figueroa married Juan de Avalos (from Sayula).
2 CONT 6.	María de Carbajal y Figueroa married Antonio Marín de Mendoza.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	The marriages of the five daughters are recorded by Fray Diego Padilla Dávila, "Arcedeán" of the Guadalajara cathedral, in his manuscript (Bancroft Library).
2 CONT 	The records of the two sons that were friars are in Inquisición, Vol. 203, expediente 7 and Vol. 846, folio 260.  Rodrigo de Carbajal y Ulloa was born in Cáceres, Cáceres, Spain, a son of Hernán Pérez de Monroy and María de
2 CONC  Ulloa.
2 CONT 	I have a copy of the issue of the Spanish magazine "Hidalguía" that has an article about the mayorazgo de Treviño in Cáceres.  The genealogy of some Carbajales is there, intermarrying with Ulloas way back.
2 CONT
2 CONT A lot has been written about Mariana Temiño de Bañuelos y Alcocer, born in Sevilla and a daughter of Diego Temiño de Bañuelos and Francisca de Alcocer.  She married twice:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1)	The Conquistador, Pedro Pacho, from Fregenal de la Sierra, Badajoz, a son of Diego Pacho and Beatriz Rodríguez.  I descend from daughters 1 and 2.
2 CONT 1.	Angela de Velasco, born c. 1540, Mexico City.  Married 1555 Captain Diego Flores de la Torre.
2 CONT 2.	Mariana de Velasco, chr. 25 Aug 1543 in ibid., married Captain Lorenzo Padilla Dávila.
2 CONT 3.	Bernardina María Temiño, chr. 26 Aug 1545 in ibid., married Captain Gaspar de la Mota y Mena.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2)	Francisco de Figueroa y Carbajal, from the "Reinos de Castilla."  Their children:
2 CONT 1.	Francisca de Figueroa y Temiño de Bañuelos, who married Rodrigo de Carbajal y Ulloa.
2 CONT 2.	Pedro de Figueroa y Temiño de Bañuelos married Beatriz de Angulo y Montesinos.
2 CONT 3.	María de Figueroa y Temiño de Bañuelos.  The "Arcedeán," Diego Padilla Dávila wrote that she married a "licenciado" who was an "Oidor" in Guatemala, and had no issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.	Diego Delgadillo and Melchora de los Reyes' sibling, Juan Rodríguez is the root of the Rodríguez de Frías branch, quite numerous in Nochistlán.  My premise that Melchora was the other two's sister is based on the following:
2 CONT 2.	It is the only Delgadillo line in Nochistlán.
2 CONT 3.	She and Rodrigo de Carbajal had two children-Hernando and Josefa-that employed the Delgadillo surname.
2 CONT 4.	Hernando was given her father's first name.
2 CONT 5.	A son, Juan, used Rodríguez in the composite surname Rodríguez de Carbajal.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Amaya Topete wrote that the Conquistador of Nueva Galicia, Francisco Delgadillo was from Santo Domingo del Val, Toledo.  He arrived after the fall of Tenochtitlán.  In 1529, he was with Nuño Beltrán de Guzmán in the Nueva Gal
2 CONC icia campaign.  He owned a "solar" in the first Guadalajara, was in the defense of the third (Nochistlán) and one of the founders of the fourth, in 1542.  Had the encomiendas of Apozol, in Juchipila and Atiataque in the Tonalá v
2 CONC alley.
2 CONT 	In "Los Conquistadores Fernández de Híjar y Bracamonte," the same author states that in Almórox, jurisdiction of Escalona, in Toledo, on 12 Jan 1540 his brother, Diego Delgadillo made an "información de nobleza."  The documen
2 CONC t says their parents were Juan Delgadillo and Ana del Valle, and the paternal grandparents Ruy González Delgadillo and Inés de Vellosilla.
2 CONT 	Francisco married Isabel de Avalos, a niece of the Conquistador, Alonso de Avalos.  Four children are named.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT So:  Diego Delgadillo was Isabel Calderón's first husband and the children's father.  Both she and Juan de Rentería lived close to a century.  He died 1 Aug 1679 and she was buried 15 Feb 1683, both in Nochistlán.  Both made wi
2 CONC lls, which might be in the Guadalajara public records and would be very val
2 CONT By dates, my conjecture is that Hernando de Frías married one of the daughters.  Diego Delgadillo and Isabel Calderón's son, Juan Delgadillo, and his spouse, María de la Concepción de Islas, had a daughter named Isabel de Vell
2 CONC osillo.  (The Conquistador's paternal grandmother's surname.)  She married Juan de Morones.
2 CONT In my other family line, the surname appears again.  Luisa Delgadillo (also Diego and Isabel's daughter) and her husband, Esteban Tejeda de los Monteros, had a daughter christened in Aguascalientes, on 5 Nov 1616 as Leonor Vellosi
2 CONC llo.  The summary:
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Ruy González Delgadillo
2 CONT 	md. Inés de Vellosilla
2 CONT 		|
2 CONT 	Juan Delgadillo
2 CONT 	md. Ana del Valle
2 CONT 		|
2 CONT 	Francisco Delgadillo, Conquistador  md. Isabel de Avalos
2 CONT 					|
2 CONT 						  "x" Delgadillo  md.  Hernando de Frías
2 CONT 		|				|				|
2 CONT 	Diego Delgadillo		Juan Rodríguez (1)		Melchora de los Reyes
2 CONT 	md. Isabel Calderón		md. Gertrudis de Amaya		md. Rodrigo de Carbajal
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT (1)	Died in Nochistlán 26 Jun 1672 at age 89. (Born 1583.) Name:  Juan Rodríguez de Frías.
2 CONT
2 CONT Where does your Melchora de los Reyes syndrome fit in?  Mine lived in Ahuacatlán, Nayarit, the wife of Rodrigo de Carvajal y Figueroa.  One of the four known offspring was Rodrigo de Carbajal y Ulloa, married in Nochistlán 12 Se
2 CONC p 1628 to Juana de Lomelín y Benavides, an ancestral line.  That Melchora de los Reyes (living on 4 June 1656, the date she was a Godmother of a Nochistlán christening, "con su yerno, Francisco Morgado") was the sister of Diego 
2 CONC Delgadillo, Isabel de Calderón's first husband (the second was Juan de Rentería).  And Melchora and Diego's father was Hernando de Frías.  Their sibling was Juan Rodríguez de Frías.  He died in Nochistlán 26 June 1672, age 8
2 CONC 9, a widower of Gertrudis de Amaya.  He was born c. 1583.  Ophelia sent the photocopy of part of the book "Relación del Pueblo de Teocaltiche" where Diego Delgadillo appears as the "alcalde mayor" and Pase a Indias for those Hern
2 CONC ando or Fernando de Frías that might be the root of this lineage
2 CONT
2 CONT So: In her first marriage, to Rodrigo de Carbajal, with residence in Ahuacatlán, Nayarit, she begat three children during the first few years of the 1600s: Rodrigo de Carbajal y Ulloa, Juan Rodríguez de Carbajal and Elvira de Ca
2 CONC rbajal y Ulloa (talked about in previous correspondence).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 	In the work of Diego Flores de la Torre, Archdeacon of the Guadalajara Cathedral, in 1668 (the Bancroft Library has the manuscript), this genealogist of his time wrote: "doña Regina, casó en el pueblo de Yahualulco con Pedro de
2 CONC  los Reyes."  The township is Ahualulco, Nayarit.  "Doña Regina was spawned by Catalina de Ibarra and her second husband, Captain Pedro de Arteaga.  Flores de la Torre takes the Ibarras back to connections with other families; th
2 CONC at isn't the theme here.  What came to my attention was Pedro de los Reyes, from Ahualulco.
2 CONT Melchora de los Reyes lived there with her first husband Rodrigo de Carbajal.  She was possibly born in Ahualulco and it was where her kin resided.  When Melchora married Luis Delgadillo, we know that she and her offspring went to
2 CONC  live in his territory: Nochistlán.  I had thought that her complete name was a given name, and wondered what the surname was, misled by the use of Melchora de los Reyes as a given name by many of her descendants.  Now I believe 
2 CONC that Melchora was her given name and de los Reyes the surname.
2 CONT 	Pedro de los Reyes, spoken of by the Archdeacon, was perhaps two generations after Melchora.  It points out that branches of the same family line were still in Ahualulco.  To trace back Melchora's ancestry, look for de los
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Next, probably was your ancestor, Melchora, born c. 1658 and married c. 1676 to Joseph de Ornelas y Mendoza.
2 CONT 	A son was Hernando Pérez de Frías (named after Hernando de Frías), whose spouse was Maria de Carbajal y Yáñez.  Hernando was gone prior to May 1718, while his widow lived in Oct 1728.  Two sons married in San Felipe Cuquío,
2 CONC  Jalisco and settled there.
2 CONT 	The fourth and last identified offspring was Catalina Cortés, married in Nochistlán 11 Nov 1691 to "Lázaro Sánchez, español vecino de Jalostotitlán."  He was a son of Lázaro Sánchez and Jerónima Rodríguez de Chavarría.
2 CONC   The "padrinos" of the ceremony were "Pedro Ramírez y María Sánchez."  They were from Jalos; she was the groom's sister and Pedro was her husband.
2 CONT 	Have a marriage "dispensa" dated 7 May 1718 (6119, AGN) in which the family tree says Diego Pérez de Frías/Pérez Delgadillo had a sister, María Delgadillo.  She gave birth to Melchora de los Reyes, who married Nicolás Vargas
2 CONC .  Their marriage was in Nochistlán, 16 Apr 1654: "Nicolás Vargas con Melchora de los Reyes, hija legítima de María Delgadillo.  Fueron los padrinos Domingo de Lomelín y Juana de Mendoza."  As I've seen was the habit at that 
2 CONC early period, the parents of the betrothed who were deceased were not mentioned.  Found data on this couple's children living in San Felipe de Cuquío.
2 CONT 	In Nochistlán, 28 Apr 1631 "casé y velé a Bernardino Gonzalez hijo de Francisco González y Magdalena de Labesares su mujer, con Mariana de Sandoval hija de Juan de Sandoval y Catalina Cortés su mujer.  Fueron los padrinos Ge
2 CONC rónimo Gonzalez Aramburu y Elvira de Carvajal y Ulloa su mujer."  These were the parents of Catalina Cortés de Isla, who wed Diego Pérez de Frías/Pérez Delgadillo.
2 CONT 	Bernardino "and company" manufactured a lot of children, and most of them married.  His death, in ibid., 5 Jan 1681: "Murió de tabardillo en la Estancia de Nangue, Bernardino de Ysla, viudo de Mariana de Sandoval, españoles, a 
2 CONC la edad de 73 años.  (His birth: Jan 1681-73 1607) Testó el 23 de octubre de 1671 ante Melchor de Sandoval, siendo albacea su esposa, difunta y herederos sus hijos e hijas."
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Following up on Rodrigo de Carbajal and his spouse, Melchora de los Reyes, residents of Ahuacatlán, Nayarit.  Putting together my notes on the Carbajales, from various sources, has borne fruit.
2 CONT 	According to Fray Antonio Tello in his history of Nueva Galicia, one of the founders of Santiago de Compostela was Rodrigo de Carbajal y Ulloa.  Amaya Topete wrote that he was a miner and land owner in Tenango and the Autlán val
2 CONC ley, with residence in Ahuacatlán, where he took part in an auction in 1579.  In 1557, he was the "Alcalde Mayor" of the Provincia de Avalos.  He still lived on 5 Apr 1606, when he was the Godfather at a christening in Guadalajar
2 CONC a.
2 CONT 	His first wife and relative, born in Mexico City, was Bernardina de Tapia, the daughter of Antonio de Carbajal and Catalina de Tapia.  Their two sons, Agustín and Diego de Carbajal y Tapia became friars in the Order of San Agust
2 CONC ín.  I'll cite their records as Aspirantes a Cargos del Santo Oficio later on.
2 CONT 	Rodrigo later married Francisca de Figueroa y Temiño de Bañuelos, a daughter of Francisco de Figueroa y Carbajal and Mariana Temiño de Bañuelos y Alcocer.  Their offspring were:
2 CONT 1.	Rodrigo de Carbajal y Figueroa, married to Melchora de los Reyes.
2 CONT 2.	Francisca de Carbajal y Figueroa, married to Juan de Ocampo (residents of Colima).
2 CONT 3.	Ana de Carbajal y Figueroa married Francisco Tello de Orozco.  Died 12 May 1620 in Guadalajara.
2 CONT 4.	Isabel de Carbajal y Figueroa wed Gabriel del Aguila.
2 CONT 5.	Catalina de Carbajal y Figueroa married Juan de Avalos (from Sayula).
2 CONT 6.	María de Carbajal y Figueroa married Antonio Marín de Mendoza.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	The marriages of the five daughters are recorded by Fray Diego Padilla Dávila, "Arcedeán" of the Guadalajara cathedral, in his manuscript (Bancroft Library).
2 CONT 	The records of the two sons that were friars are in Inquisición, Vol. 203, expediente 7 and Vol. 846, folio 260.  Rodrigo de Carbajal y Ulloa was born in Cáceres, Cáceres, Spain, a son of Hernán Pérez de Monroy and María de
2 CONC  Ulloa.
2 CONT 	I have a copy of the issue of the Spanish magazine "Hidalguía" that has an article about the mayorazgo de Treviño in Cáceres.  The genealogy of some Carbajales is there, intermarrying with Ulloas way back.
2 CONT
2 CONT A lot has been written about Mariana Temiño de Bañuelos y Alcocer, born in Sevilla and a daughter of Diego Temiño de Bañuelos and Francisca de Alcocer.  She married twice:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1)	The Conquistador, Pedro Pacho, from Fregenal de la Sierra, Badajoz, a son of Diego Pacho and Beatriz Rodríguez.  I descend from daughters 1 and 2.
2 CONT 1.	Angela de Velasco, born c. 1540, Mexico City.  Married 1555 Captain Diego Flores de la Torre.
2 CONT 2.	Mariana de Velasco, chr. 25 Aug 1543 in ibid., married Captain Lorenzo Padilla Dávila.
2 CONT 3.	Bernardina María Temiño, chr. 26 Aug 1545 in ibid., married Captain Gaspar de la Mota y Mena.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2)	Francisco de Figueroa y Carbajal, from the "Reinos de Castilla."  Their children:
2 CONT 1.	Francisca de Figueroa y Temiño de Bañuelos, who married Rodrigo de Carbajal y Ulloa.
2 CONT 2.	Pedro de Figueroa y Temiño de Bañuelos married Beatriz de Angulo y Montesinos.
2 CONT 3.	María de Figueroa y Temiño de Bañuelos.  The "Arcedeán," Diego Padilla Dávila wrote that she married a "licenciado" who was an "Oidor" in Guatemala, and had no issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.	Diego Delgadillo and Melchora de los Reyes' sibling, Juan Rodríguez is the root of the Rodríguez de Frías branch, quite numerous in Nochistlán.  My premise that Melchora was the other two's sister is based on the following:
2 CONT 2.	It is the only Delgadillo line in Nochistlán.
2 CONT 3.	She and Rodrigo de Carbajal had two children-Hernando and Josefa-that employed the Delgadillo surname.
2 CONT 4.	Hernando was given her father's first name.
2 CONT 5.	A son, Juan, used Rodríguez in the composite surname Rodríguez de Carbajal.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Amaya Topete wrote that the Conquistador of Nueva Galicia, Francisco Delgadillo was from Santo Domingo del Val, Toledo.  He arrived after the fall of Tenochtitlán.  In 1529, he was with Nuño Beltrán de Guzmán in the Nueva Gal
2 CONC icia campaign.  He owned a "solar" in the first Guadalajara, was in the defense of the third (Nochistlán) and one of the founders of the fourth, in 1542.  Had the encomiendas of Apozol, in Juchipila and Atiataque in the Tonalá v
2 CONC alley.
2 CONT 	In "Los Conquistadores Fernández de Híjar y Bracamonte," the same author states that in Almórox, jurisdiction of Escalona, in Toledo, on 12 Jan 1540 his brother, Diego Delgadillo made an "información de nobleza."  The documen
2 CONC t says their parents were Juan Delgadillo and Ana del Valle, and the paternal grandparents Ruy González Delgadillo and Inés de Vellosilla.
2 CONT 	Francisco married Isabel de Avalos, a niece of the Conquistador, Alonso de Avalos.  Four children are named.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT So:  Diego Delgadillo was Isabel Calderón's first husband and the children's father.  Both she and Juan de Rentería lived close to a century.  He died 1 Aug 1679 and she was buried 15 Feb 1683, both in Nochistlán.  Both made wi
2 CONC lls, which might be in the Guadalajara public records and would be very val
2 CONT By dates, my conjecture is that Hernando de Frías married one of the daughters.  Diego Delgadillo and Isabel Calderón's son, Juan Delgadillo, and his spouse, María de la Concepción de Islas, had a daughter named Isabel de Vell
2 CONC osillo.  (The Conquistador's paternal grandmother's surname.)  She married Juan de Morones.
2 CONT In my other family line, the surname appears again.  Luisa Delgadillo (also Diego and Isabel's daughter) and her husband, Esteban Tejeda de los Monteros, had a daughter christened in Aguascalientes, on 5 Nov 1616 as Leonor Vellosi
2 CONC llo.  The summary:
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Ruy González Delgadillo
2 CONT 	md. Inés de Vellosilla
2 CONT 		|
2 CONT 	Juan Delgadillo
2 CONT 	md. Ana del Valle
2 CONT 		|
2 CONT 	Francisco Delgadillo, Conquistador  md. Isabel de Avalos
2 CONT 					|
2 CONT 						  "x" Delgadillo  md.  Hernando de Frías
2 CONT 		|				|				|
2 CONT 	Diego Delgadillo		Juan Rodríguez (1)		Melchora de los Reyes
2 CONT 	md. Isabel Calderón		md. Gertrudis de Amaya		md. Rodrigo de Carbajal
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT (1)	Died in Nochistlán 26 Jun 1672 at age 89. (Born 1583.) Name:  Juan Rodríguez de Frías.
2 CONT
2 CONT Where does your Melchora de los Reyes syndrome fit in?  Mine lived in Ahuacatlán, Nayarit, the wife of Rodrigo de Carvajal y Figueroa.  One of the four known offspring was Rodrigo de Carbajal y Ulloa, married in Nochistlán 12 Se
2 CONC p 1628 to Juana de Lomelín y Benavides, an ancestral line.  That Melchora de los Reyes (living on 4 June 1656, the date she was a Godmother of a Nochistlán christening, "con su yerno, Francisco Morgado") was the sister of Diego 
2 CONC Delgadillo, Isabel de Calderón's first husband (the second was Juan de Rentería).  And Melchora and Diego's father was Hernando de Frías.  Their sibling was Juan Rodríguez de Frías.  He died in Nochistlán 26 June 1672, age 8
2 CONC 9, a widower of Gertrudis de Amaya.  He was born c. 1583.  Ophelia sent the photocopy of part of the book "Relación del Pueblo de Teocaltiche" where Diego Delgadillo appears as the "alcalde mayor" and Pase a Indias for those Hern
2 CONC ando or Fernando de Frías that might be the root of this lineage
2 CONT
2 CONT So: In her first marriage, to Rodrigo de Carbajal, with residence in Ahuacatlán, Nayarit, she begat three children during the first few years of the 1600s: Rodrigo de Carbajal y Ulloa, Juan Rodríguez de Carbajal and Elvira de Ca
2 CONC rbajal y Ulloa (talked about in previous correspondence).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 	In the work of Diego Flores de la Torre, Archdeacon of the Guadalajara Cathedral, in 1668 (the Bancroft Library has the manuscript), this genealogist of his time wrote: "doña Regina, casó en el pueblo de Yahualulco con Pedro de
2 CONC  los Reyes."  The township is Ahualulco, Nayarit.  "Doña Regina was spawned by Catalina de Ibarra and her second husband, Captain Pedro de Arteaga.  Flores de la Torre takes the Ibarras back to connections with other families; th
